        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Isopycnic Ocean Model (Oberhuber, 1993)",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "turbulent mixed layer",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "The top layer of the model represents the turbulent mixed layer at the ocean surface in which atmospheric processes (wind stresses and surface heat fluxes) directly force the model.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Aleutian Low",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Pacific Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "During the 1976-77 winter season, the atmosphere-ocean climate system over the North Pacific Ocean was observed to shift its basic state abruptly (e.g., Graham, 1994). The Aleutian Low deepened (Fig. 1a) causing the storm tracks to shift southward and to increase storm intensity.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "storm",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "southeastern U.S.",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Downstream, over the continent of North America, warmer temperatures occurred in the northwest (Folland and Parker, 1990), decreased storminess was observed in the southeastern U.S. (Trenberth and Hurrell, 1993), and diminished precipitation and streamflow in the western U.S. (Cayan and Peterson, 1989).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "atmospheric teleconnections",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "midlatitudes",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "It has been suggested independently by several investigators that the '76-'77 shift was caused by remote forcing from the tropical Pacific Ocean, where a contemporaneous warming of SST occurred, via well-known atmospheric teleconnections to the midlatitudes (Graham, 1994).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Ocean currents",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Pacific Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The changed heat flux pattern alone cannot entirely explain the SST shift in either the model or the observations, as is evident in the far western Pacific and in the positioning of the mid-Pacific SST cooling with respect to the heat flux shift in Figure 1. Ocean currents were found to be the important additional effect which caused the shift.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Ekman currents",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "eastern and central North Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "This depiction of events is consistent with previous ocean modeling results by Haney (1980) for the September 1976 through January 1977 time interval, which showed that SST anomalies in the eastern and central North Pacific were predominantly due to anomalous heat fluxes and anomalous Ekman currents, respectively.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
